  { q: 'What is the significance of ravens in Tibetan Buddhism?', a: 'In Tibetan Buddhist tradition, the raven (bya rog in Tibetan, though the same word is used for crows) is closely associated with Mahakala (mGon po), one of the most important Dharma-protector deities (dharmapala) in Vajrayana Buddhism. Mahakala in one of his many forms — particularly Brahmarupa Mahakala — has the raven as his mount or manifestation. The raven-crow\'s appearance near a monastery or stupa can be interpreted as the presence of Mahakala, and in some Tibetan communities ravens are not harmed or disturbed as a consequence.' },
  { q: 'Is the raven considered sacred in Tibet?', a: 'The raven and crow are associated with protective and sometimes wrathful supernatural forces in Tibetan tradition, which creates an attitude of respectful wariness rather than the hostility common in many agricultural societies. In the context of Mahakala as a dharmapala (protector of the Dharma), the birds associated with him are treated as his emanations. Some monasteries in Tibet and Bhutan have traditionally protected ravens nesting on or near the monastery as manifestations of Mahakala\'s presence. This attitude varies regionally and by specific tradition.' },
  { q: 'Are there raven divination traditions in Tibet?', a: 'Yes. Tibetan corvid divination (using the direction and character of calls from crows and ravens) is part of the broader Tibetan system of divination that blends Bon (pre-Buddhist Tibetan religion) and Buddhist elements. The Bön tradition — Tibet\'s indigenous shamanistic/animistic religion — has rich bird-omen traditions predating Buddhism. The raven\'s call direction, timing, and number of calls are interpreted in some traditional contexts as omens of travel, visitors, or events. This continues in folk belief even where formal Bon ritual practice has declined.' },
];
